## Proof

We claim the answer is **yes**: $\int_t^\infty \exp(-x+o(x))\,dx = \exp(-t+o(t))$ as $t\to\infty$.

**Setup.** Write the integrand as $f(x)=e^{-x+g(x)}$ where $g(x)=o(x)$ as $x\to\infty$, i.e. $g(x)/x\to 0$. Note $f$ is integrable on $[T,\infty)$ for large $T$: choosing $\varepsilon<1$, for $x$ large enough $g(x)\le \varepsilon x$, so $f(x)\le e^{-(1-\varepsilon)x}$.

**Substitution.** Set $u=x-t$:
$$I(t):=\int_t^\infty e^{-x+g(x)}\,dx = e^{-t}\int_0^\infty e^{-u+g(t+u)}\,du =: e^{-t}\,J(t).$$
Thus $\log I(t) = -t + \log J(t)$, and it suffices to prove $\log J(t)=o(t)$.

**Bounding $J(t)$.** Fix $\varepsilon>0$ (with $\varepsilon<1$ for the upper bound). Since $g(x)=o(x)$, there is $X$ such that $|g(x)|\le \varepsilon x$ for all $x\ge X$. For $t\ge X$ and every $u\ge 0$ we have $t+u\ge X$, hence $-\varepsilon(t+u)\le g(t+u)\le \varepsilon(t+u)$.

*Upper bound:*
$$J(t)\le \int_0^\infty e^{-u+\varepsilon(t+u)}\,du = e^{\varepsilon t}\int_0^\infty e^{-(1-\varepsilon)u}\,du = \frac{e^{\varepsilon t}}{1-\varepsilon}.$$
So $\log J(t)\le \varepsilon t + \log\!\tfrac{1}{1-\varepsilon}\le \varepsilon t + C_\varepsilon$.

*Lower bound:*
$$J(t)\ge \int_0^\infty e^{-u-\varepsilon(t+u)}\,du = e^{-\varepsilon t}\int_0^\infty e^{-(1+\varepsilon)u}\,du = \frac{e^{-\varepsilon t}}{1+\varepsilon}.$$
So $\log J(t)\ge -\varepsilon t - \log(1+\varepsilon)\ge -\varepsilon t - C'_\varepsilon$.

**Conclusion.** For all $t\ge X$,
$$-\varepsilon t - C'_\varepsilon \;\le\; \log J(t)\;\le\; \varepsilon t + C_\varepsilon.$$
Since $\varepsilon>0$ was arbitrary, $\frac{\log J(t)}{t}\to 0$, i.e. $\log J(t)=o(t)$, equivalently $J(t)=e^{o(t)}$. Therefore
$$I(t)=e^{-t}\,e^{o(t)}=\exp(-t+o(t)),$$
which is exactly the claimed asymptotic. $\blacksquare$

